INSERT INTO pages_description (page_id, title, description, seo_description, seo_title, language)
SELECT id, 'Dental Implant Treatment',
'<p>When a tooth is lost, the gap it leaves does not simply stay as it is. Neighbouring teeth may gradually tilt into the space, the opposing tooth may over-erupt, and the chewing load is distributed unevenly onto the remaining teeth. The jawbone in that area may also lose volume over time, because the load it used to carry has gone. For this reason, replacing a missing tooth is often not merely a question of appearance.</p>
<p>A dental implant is an artificial tooth root placed in the jawbone in place of the missing tooth. It can carry a single crown, a bridge replacing several teeth, or a fixed full-arch prosthesis. The function of the implant is to take the place of the lost root and support the prosthesis from the bone.</p>
<h2>Not every missing tooth is replaced with an implant</h2>
<p>An implant is not the only option for treating a missing tooth. A tooth-supported bridge, a removable partial denture and, in some cases, orthodontic space closure may also be considered. Which option is appropriate is determined by assessing together the position and size of the gap, the condition of the neighbouring teeth, the volume and quality of the jawbone, gum health, the bite relationship, general health and the person''s daily oral care habits.</p>
<p>This assessment is made through examination, the necessary imaging and prosthetic planning. Any definitive statement of suitability given over the telephone, by message or from a photograph is not reliable.</p>
<h2>Treatment is a process, not a single procedure</h2>
<p>Implant treatment generally consists of the following stages: examination and imaging, drawing up the treatment plan, any preparatory procedures required, placement of the implant, the healing period during which the implant integrates with the bone, fabrication of the restoration, and regular check-ups.</p>
<p>The duration of these stages varies markedly from person to person. A case in which bone volume is sufficient does not follow the same timetable as one requiring bone augmentation. For this reason no fixed timescale is promised; the estimated stages are shared after the examination.</p>
<h2>Long-term outcome depends on more than one factor</h2>
<p>Whether an implant functions for many years depends on appropriate case selection, planning, the surgical and prosthetic work, the person''s oral hygiene, smoking, general health and regular check-ups. If any one of these is overlooked, the outcome may be affected. The tissues around an implant can become inflamed just as they do around natural teeth; care does not end when the treatment ends.</p>
<h2>What is assessed before an implant?</h2>
<p>The examination does not look only at the area of the missing tooth. The condition of the remaining teeth, gum health, the bite relationship and any clenching or grinding habit are assessed. If gum disease is present it is treated before the implant, because the tissues around an implant are affected by the same bacterial plaque.</p>
<p>Medical history is also part of the planning. Uncontrolled diabetes, certain medicines affecting bone metabolism, bleeding disorders, previous radiotherapy and smoking are all factors that may affect healing. None of these is in itself an absolute barrier, but they may change the plan and the expected course. It is therefore important to tell us about the medicines you take and any known conditions at the first consultation.</p>
<h2>The prosthesis is not an afterthought</h2>
<p>A common misconception is that the implant is placed first and the tooth to be fitted on it is planned afterwards. In fact the order is the reverse: which tooth is to be made, in what form and in what position is decided first, and the position and angle of the implant are chosen according to that plan. This is known as prosthetically driven planning.</p>
<p>The practical meaning of this approach is as follows: an implant that has not been correctly positioned may not allow a well-shaped, cleanable and aesthetic tooth to be made on it, even if it integrates with the bone without any problem. The planning stage is therefore no less important than the surgical stage.</p>
<h2>Pages in this section</h2>
<h3>Starting implant treatment</h3>
<ul>
<li><a href="/en/dental-implant-tedavisi/">Dental Implant Treatment</a> — What an implant does, for whom it may be considered, the stages of treatment and the healing process.</li>
<li>Options for Replacing Missing Teeth — The differences between implants, bridges and removable dentures. <em>(in preparation)</em></li>
<li>Implant Treatment Plan and Scope — What determines the treatment plan. <em>(in preparation)</em></li>
<li>When Can an Implant Be Placed After Extraction? — The criteria that determine timing. <em>(in preparation)</em></li>
</ul>
<h3>When bone is insufficient</h3>
<ul>
<li>Bone Grafting and Bone Augmentation — Methods considered when bone volume is insufficient. <em>(in preparation)</em></li>
<li>Sinus Lifting — The approach used when bone height is limited in the posterior upper jaw. <em>(in preparation)</em></li>
</ul>
<h3>Restorations and prosthetics</h3>
<ul>
<li>Fixed Implant-Supported Prostheses — Single crowns, bridges and fixed full-arch options. <em>(in preparation)</em></li>
<li>All-on-4 and All-on-6 — Fixed prostheses on a limited number of implants in a full arch. <em>(in preparation)</em></li>
</ul>
<h3>The procedure and healing</h3>
<ul>
<li>Is Implant Treatment Painful? — Local anaesthesia and the healing period. <em>(in preparation)</em></li>
<li>The First Days After an Implant — Pain, swelling, bleeding, eating and aftercare. <em>(in preparation)</em></li>
</ul>
<h3>Long-term care</h3>
<ul>
<li>How Are Implants Cleaned? — The daily cleaning routine and interdental brushes. <em>(in preparation)</em></li>
<li>Implants in Smokers and People with Diabetes — How these two factors affect healing. <em>(in preparation)</em></li>
</ul>
<h3>If problems arise</h3>
<ul>
<li>Why Do Implants Fail? — The causes of early and late implant loss. <em>(in preparation)</em></li>
<li>Peri-implantitis — Inflammation and bone loss around an implant. <em>(in preparation)</em></li>
</ul>
<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
<p><strong>Can implants be placed in anyone?</strong><br>Suitability is determined by assessing together the volume and quality of the jawbone, gum health, general health, the medicines taken, smoking and oral hygiene. No decision can be made without an examination and imaging.</p>
<p><strong>Where should I start?</strong><br>The first step is an examination. Once your complaint, the intraoral findings and the necessary imaging have been assessed together, the options and the estimated stages are shared with you.</p>
<p><strong>How many implants will I need?</strong><br>The number of missing teeth is not the only determining factor. Bone distribution, the bite load, the type of prosthesis planned and aesthetic requirements are all assessed together. No number is committed to before an examination.</p>
<p><strong>Is any special care needed after having an implant?</strong><br>Yes. The tissues around an implant can become inflamed. Daily cleaning, interdental cleaning and regular check-ups have a direct effect on the long-term outcome.</p>
<p><strong>Is my age a barrier to implants?</strong><br>There is no upper age limit; what matters is not age but bone and gum support together with general health. At the lower end, jaw growth is expected to be complete.</p>
<h2>Local assessment</h2>
<p>At our clinic at the Beylikdüzü–Esenyurt junction, suitability for implants is determined by assessing the clinical examination, the necessary imaging findings and the prosthetic plan together. Following the assessment, the options and estimated stages are shared with you in writing.</p>
<p><a href="/en/agiz-cerrahisi-uygulamalari/">Oral Surgery Procedures</a> &middot; <a href="/en/iletisim">Appointments and contact</a></p>',
'What is implant treatment, who is it suitable for and what stages does it involve? Planning, surgery, prosthetics and long-term care explained.',
'Dental Implant Treatment | MSc. Dt. Mehmet Taş',
'EN' FROM pages WHERE link = 'implant';

INSERT INTO pages_description (page_id, title, description, seo_description, seo_title, language)
SELECT id, 'Aesthetic Dentistry',
'<p>Aesthetic dentistry aims to bring the colour, shape and alignment of the teeth into harmony with the face. But the foundation on which this work rests is always oral health. If there is active decay, gum inflammation, an untreated root problem or an unbalanced bite, aesthetic work is not carried out on top of it; those problems are addressed first.</p>
<p>The reason is not simply that this is the rule. The margin of a restoration placed at the edge of inflamed gum tissue may become exposed over time, and if a clenching habit is overlooked, newly placed ceramic may be damaged earlier than expected. Aesthetic planning therefore begins with an examination.</p>
<h2>No single method suits everyone</h2>
<p>Two people presenting with the same complaint may not be offered the same treatment. For a tooth that has darkened, whitening is sometimes enough; at other times there is an underlying cause and a restorative solution is needed. Bonding may be sufficient for a small irregularity in shape, whereas a crown comes into question where there has been extensive loss of tooth substance. For problems of alignment, orthodontic assessment may be more conservative than a restorative procedure.</p>
<p>The following are assessed together when deciding: the remaining sound tooth structure, the amount of enamel, the position of the tooth in the mouth, the underlying colour, gum levels, bite forces, any clenching or grinding habit, and the person''s expectations. The aim is to reach the result by the most conservative means available.</p>
<h2>How is a natural appearance planned?</h2>
<p>A natural result comes from the harmony of the whole, not from individual teeth. Tooth proportions, surface texture, translucency, the course of the gum margin, the smile line and the relationship between lips and teeth are all considered together. Simply whitening the teeth, or making them all the same size, does not produce a natural appearance; what is decisive is the relationship established with the neighbouring teeth and with the face.</p>
<p>The gums are part of this whole. How much gum shows when you smile, the symmetry of the levels and the health of the tissue are as influential as the teeth in whether the result is perceived as natural.</p>
<h2>Expectations and limits are discussed openly</h2>
<p>One of the most important stages in aesthetic treatment is sharing expectations before the work begins. In suitable cases, digital planning and a preview can give an idea of the possible outcome. A preview is a planning tool; it is not a commitment to an identical result.</p>
<p>Ceramic and composite materials behave differently from natural teeth in terms of colour: a natural tooth changes colour over time while a restoration may stay the same. For this reason, if whitening and restorative work are planned together, the order matters. Equally, no fixed lifespan can be given for any restoration; the outcome is determined as much by care, the bite and check-ups as by the choice of material.</p>
<h2>The order changes the result</h2>
<p>In aesthetic treatment, the order in which procedures are carried out is as important as which procedures are carried out. The general approach is as follows: oral health problems are resolved first, alignment is corrected if needed, colour work is then carried out, and restorative procedures are completed last.</p>
<p>The reason for this order is practical. If the teeth are to be corrected orthodontically, crowns placed before that may not fit the new position. If whitening is planned, restorations in the visible area should be chosen according to the shade achieved, because ceramic and composite are not affected by whitening.</p>
<h2>When is less better?</h2>
<p>In aesthetic dentistry the best result does not always come from the most extensive procedure. Once sound enamel has been removed it does not grow back; so if the same result can be reached by a more conservative route, that route is preferred.</p>
<p>For example: bonding may be considered instead of a crown for a small fracture in a single tooth; whitening may be sufficient instead of a restoration for a mild colour complaint; a gap between teeth can in some cases be closed orthodontically. Not every one of these options is appropriate in every case, but they should be considered together at the examination. If you are offered only one option, you are entitled to ask why the alternatives are not suitable.</p>
<h2>Pages in this section</h2>
<h3>Planning</h3>
<ul>
<li><a href="/en/gulus-tasarimi/">Personalised Smile Design</a> — The planning process in which teeth, gums, lips and facial proportions are assessed together.</li>
<li>Proportion in Smile Design — Where the rules of proportion are useful. <em>(in preparation)</em></li>
<li>Pink Aesthetics and Gum Contouring — Gum levels and symmetry. <em>(in preparation)</em></li>
</ul>
<h3>Crowns and veneers</h3>
<ul>
<li><a href="/en/zirkonyum-dis-kaplama/">Zirconia Crowns</a> — Where they are used, the digital workflow, material options and care.</li>
<li>E.max and Laminate Veneers — Suitability for lithium disilicate and laminate work. <em>(in preparation)</em></li>
<li>Veneer or Zirconia? — A comparison of the two options. <em>(in preparation)</em></li>
</ul>
<h3>More conservative options</h3>
<ul>
<li>Bonding and Aesthetic Composite Fillings — A conservative approach for limited changes in shape and colour. <em>(in preparation)</em></li>
<li><a href="/en/dis-beyazlatma/">Teeth Whitening</a> — In-office, home and combined methods; managing sensitivity.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
<p><strong>Where should I start, and which treatment do I need?</strong><br>The starting point is a smile design assessment. Once your expectations, the oral health findings and the bite have been examined together, we explain which procedures are appropriate and whether a more conservative alternative exists.</p>
<p><strong>Do my teeth have to be prepared?</strong><br>No. In some cases options that require no preparation, such as whitening or bonding, may be sufficient. The amount of preparation depends on the existing structure of the tooth and the restoration chosen.</p>
<p><strong>Whitening first, or crowns first?</strong><br>If the natural teeth are to be whitened, restorations in the visible area are usually made after whitening and matched to the shade achieved. Restorative materials do not change colour in the way natural teeth do.</p>
<p><strong>How long will the result last?</strong><br>No fixed period can be given. The choice of material, the remaining tooth structure, the bite load, any clenching or grinding habit, oral hygiene and regular check-ups all affect the outcome.</p>
<p><strong>I grind my teeth — can aesthetic work still be done?</strong><br>A clenching or grinding habit affects the planning and is assessed first. Where necessary, a protective night guard becomes part of the plan.</p>
<h2>Local assessment</h2>
<p>At our clinic at the Beylikdüzü–Esenyurt junction, aesthetic treatment options are assessed together with oral health, preservation of tooth structure, the bite relationship and the goal of a natural appearance.</p>
<p><a href="/en/iletisim">Appointments and contact</a></p>',
'Smile design, zirconia, veneers, bonding and whitening: see which option is considered in which situation, and why the order matters.',
'Aesthetic Dentistry | MSc. Dt. Mehmet Taş',
'EN' FROM pages WHERE link = 'estetik';


INSERT INTO pages_description (page_id, title, description, seo_description, seo_title, language)
SELECT id, 'Oral and Dental Health',
'<p>People usually wait for pain before seeing a dentist. Yet in many cases pain is not the beginning of the process but an advanced stage of it. Decay does not hurt while it is confined to the enamel; gingivitis, the early stage of gum disease, may show no sign other than bleeding. Regular check-ups therefore make it possible to complete treatment with a smaller intervention and less loss of tooth structure.</p>
<p>This section covers the points where oral and dental health meets daily life: decay and fillings, gum problems, pain and emergencies, sensitivity, jaw and muscle complaints, daily care habits and children''s dental health.</p>
<h2>The mouth is not separate from the rest of the body</h2>
<p>The relationship between gum disease and diabetes works in both directions: uncontrolled blood sugar can affect the gum tissue, while ongoing gum inflammation can make glycaemic control more difficult. Similarly, hormonal changes in pregnancy can make the gums more sensitive to plaque. Some medicines cause dry mouth and can thereby increase the risk of decay.</p>
<p>For this reason an examination does not look at the teeth alone. Medical history, the medicines taken and any systemic conditions are part of the planning. Where necessary we coordinate with the relevant doctor.</p>
<h2>Daily care is the continuation of treatment</h2>
<p>No procedure carried out in the clinic replaces care at home. If plaque continues to build up after scaling, gum inflammation returns; if the risk of decay is not managed in a mouth that has been filled, new decay develops. Brushing technique, interdental cleaning, the choice of toothpaste and dietary habits are therefore as important as the treatment itself.</p>
<p>The right tool is not the same for everyone. The width of the interdental spaces, the presence of a prosthesis or implant, manual dexterity and habits may all call for different tools.</p>
<h2>Waiting is harmful in pain and emergencies</h2>
<p>Severe toothache, facial swelling, fever, a fracture after trauma or a tooth knocked out of its socket are situations that should not be left. With a permanent tooth that has been knocked out in particular, the first minutes are decisive. Dental trauma in children requires an entirely different approach depending on whether a primary or a permanent tooth is involved.</p>
<h2>The line between treatment and prevention</h2>
<p>Most procedures in dentistry fill the place of tissue that has been lost. A filling replaces decayed tissue; a crown encases a fractured tooth. But none of them is an exact substitute for natural tissue, and none lasts for ever. The real gain in dentistry therefore comes less from the quality of the procedure than from never needing it at all.</p>
<p>Prevention consists of concrete things: correct brushing technique, interdental cleaning, reducing how often sugar is consumed, making use of fluoride, and attending check-ups at intervals appropriate to your level of risk. These look simple, but their effects accumulate.</p>
<h2>Why is the approach different in children?</h2>
<p>Paediatric dentistry is not a scaled-down version of adult treatment. The enamel of primary teeth is thinner and decay can progress more quickly. A primary tooth lost early can narrow the space reserved for the permanent tooth beneath it. In trauma, primary and permanent teeth are handled in completely different ways; a primary tooth that has been knocked out is not replanted.</p>
<p>Beyond this, success in treating a child also depends on the relationship the child forms with the process. A child who first attends while free of pain, and who knows the clinic, is usually easier to manage when treatment is later needed. The purpose of the first visit is therefore generally not treatment.</p>
<h2>Pages in this section</h2>
<h3>Decay, fillings and root canals</h3>
<ul>
<li><a href="/en/kanal-tedavisi/">Root Canal Treatment</a> — When it is carried out, its stages and the restoration that follows.</li>
<li><a href="/en/dis-curugu-kompozit-dolgu/">Tooth Decay and Composite Fillings</a> — Recognising decay and the treatment options.</li>
</ul>
<h3>The first step and check-ups</h3>
<ul>
<li><a href="/en/ilk-dis-muayenesi-tedavi-planlamasi/">First Examination and Treatment Planning</a> — What is assessed at an examination.</li>
<li>Regular Check-ups — The factors that determine how often you should attend. <em>(in preparation)</em></li>
<li>Dental Anxiety — The approach to treatment in anxious patients. <em>(in preparation)</em></li>
</ul>
<h3>Gums</h3>
<ul>
<li>Scaling and Gum Health — How calculus forms and what cleaning involves. <em>(in preparation)</em></li>
<li>Gum Diseases — The distinction between gingivitis and periodontitis. <em>(in preparation)</em></li>
</ul>
<h3>Pain, sensitivity and emergencies</h3>
<ul>
<li>Dental Emergencies — When not to wait, and what to do first. <em>(in preparation)</em></li>
<li>Tooth Sensitivity — The causes of sensitivity to cold and heat. <em>(in preparation)</em></li>
</ul>
<h3>Children and special periods</h3>
<ul>
<li><a href="/en/cocuk-dis-hekimligi/">Paediatric Dentistry</a> — Age at first examination, decay in primary teeth, fluoride.</li>
<li><a href="/en/hamilelikte-dis-tedavisi/">Dental Treatment in Pregnancy</a> — Planning by trimester and care at home.</li>
<li><a href="/en/cocuklarda-dis-travmasi/">Dental Trauma in Children</a> — The first 30 minutes after a tooth is fractured or knocked out.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
<p><strong>How often should I come for a check-up?</strong><br>There is no single interval that suits everyone. Decay risk, the condition of the gums, oral hygiene, smoking and systemic conditions are assessed to set an interval tailored to you.</p>
<p><strong>I have no pain — do I still need an examination?</strong><br>Yes. Early decay and the onset of gum disease are usually painless. A problem noticed early can be treated with a smaller procedure.</p>
<p><strong>My gums bleed when I brush; should I stop brushing?</strong><br>No. In most plaque-related conditions, stopping cleaning makes the inflammation worse. Continue cleaning gently and regularly with a soft-bristled brush, and make an appointment for a check-up.</p>
<p><strong>When should I first bring my child?</strong><br>Within six months of the first primary tooth appearing, and by the first birthday at the latest. The purpose of this visit is usually not treatment but risk assessment and familiarisation.</p>
<p><strong>Which toothpaste should I use?</strong><br>The choice depends on age, decay risk, sensitivity and the condition of the gums. We explain what is suitable for you at the examination.</p>
<h2>Local assessment</h2>
<p>At our clinic at the Beylikdüzü–Esenyurt junction, oral and dental health is assessed by considering your complaints, the intraoral findings and the necessary imaging together; a personalised care routine and check-up interval are then set.</p>
<p><a href="/en/iletisim">Appointments and contact</a></p>',
'Decay, gums, toothache, sensitivity, daily care and children''s dental health: guide pages from examination through to preventive treatment.',
'Oral and Dental Health | MSc. Dt. Mehmet Taş',
'EN' FROM pages WHERE link = 'agiz-dis-sagligi';


INSERT INTO pages_description (page_id, title, description, seo_description, seo_title, language)
SELECT id, 'Oral and Maxillofacial Surgery',
'<p>Oral surgery procedures require the tooth, the jawbone and the surrounding structures to be assessed together. The position of a tooth, the shape of its roots, its relationship with the bone and the anatomical structures next to it only become clear through clinical examination and appropriate imaging. A surgical procedure is therefore not planned by looking into the mouth and saying that a tooth “needs to come out”.</p>
<p>In the lower jaw, the relationship between the roots of the tooth and the mandibular canal, and in the upper jaw the proximity of the teeth to the sinus cavity, directly affect the planning. In some cases these relationships are assessed with a panoramic radiograph, in others with three-dimensional imaging.</p>
<h2>Not every impacted tooth is extracted</h2>
<p>When wisdom teeth cannot find room in the mouth, they may erupt only partly or remain impacted within the bone. This does not always call for extraction. Extraction is considered in situations such as pain, recurrent infection, damage to the neighbouring tooth, suspicion of a cyst, a position that cannot be cleaned, or the requirements of another planned treatment. For teeth that cause no complaint and can be monitored, observation may be preferred.</p>
<p>The same approach applies to surgical extraction: whether the tooth can be saved is assessed first. Where the tooth can be kept in the mouth through root canal treatment, apical surgery or a restorative solution, extraction is not the first option.</p>
<h2>Risks are explained from the outset</h2>
<p>Swelling, tenderness, limited mouth opening and difficulty chewing are expected findings after surgical procedures. These are not complications but an ordinary part of healing, and they are usually most noticeable in the first few days. Beyond this, every procedure has its own specific risks; these include risks related to anatomical proximity, bleeding, infection and healing that progresses more slowly than expected.</p>
<p>Explaining these risks in a way that applies to you is part of the procedure. Which findings are expected and which call for prompt attention are explained both before and after the procedure.</p>
<h2>Bone volume is part of implant planning</h2>
<p>A significant part of oral surgery is bound up with implant treatment. If a tooth was lost some time ago, bone volume in that area may have decreased. In such cases bone augmentation, or elevation of the sinus floor in the posterior upper jaw, may be considered before the implant. These are not needed in every patient; the need is determined by assessing imaging and the prosthetic plan together.</p>
<h2>The planning process is explained clearly</h2>
<p>The scope and conditions of every surgical procedure differ. Following assessment, we determine under what conditions and with which team the procedure should be carried out; this planning is explained openly to the patient and the process is agreed together.</p>
<p>After his training at Istanbul University Faculty of Dentistry, MSc. Dt. Mehmet Taş completed a master''s degree in Oral Surgery and Implantology in Italy. This qualification is not a specialist title; it forms the scientific basis of the approach followed in surgical and implant planning.</p>
<h2>Preparation before the procedure</h2>
<p>There is information that needs to be shared before a surgical procedure: known medical conditions, medicines taken regularly, allergies, previous operations and any bleeding-related condition. Anticoagulant medicines, medicines affecting bone metabolism and immunosuppressive treatments directly affect the planning.</p>
<p>These medicines must not be stopped without medical advice. Where necessary, we coordinate with the relevant doctor and set the timing and method of the procedure accordingly.</p>
<h2>What to expect during healing</h2>
<p>The first days of healing follow a different course from the weeks that follow. The first 24 hours matter for protecting the blood clot: avoid disturbing the area, do not rinse vigorously, do not drink through a straw and avoid smoking. Swelling usually increases in the first few days and then subsides. There may be temporary restriction in mouth opening.</p>
<p>These are expected findings. By contrast, pain that increases and does not settle, high fever, widespread swelling, a bad smell or discharge, or difficulty swallowing or breathing require prompt attention.</p>
<h2>Pages in this section</h2>
<ul>
<li><a href="/en/agiz-cerrahisi-uygulamalari/">Oral Surgery Procedures</a> — Scope, assessment before the procedure, healing and explanation of risks.</li>
<li>Impacted Wisdom Teeth — When extraction is considered and when the tooth is monitored. <em>(in preparation)</em></li>
<li>Bone Grafting and Bone Augmentation — Methods and healing where bone is insufficient. <em>(in preparation)</em></li>
<li>Sinus Lifting — The approach used when bone height is limited in the posterior upper jaw. <em>(in preparation)</em></li>
</ul>
<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
<p><strong>My wisdom tooth does not hurt — should I still have it out?</strong><br>If there is no complaint, extraction is not automatically necessary. The position of the tooth, how easily it can be cleaned, its effect on the neighbouring tooth and the radiographic findings are assessed to decide whether extraction or monitoring is appropriate.</p>
<p><strong>Will I feel pain during the procedure?</strong><br>The procedure is carried out under local anaesthesia and comfort is the aim. You may feel pressure and vibration. As individual experience varies, no guarantee of complete absence of pain is given.</p>
<p><strong>How long does the swelling last?</strong><br>Swelling is usually most noticeable in the first few days and then subsides. The duration depends on the extent of the procedure and on the individual. With increasing pain, fever or widespread swelling, you should seek attention without waiting.</p>
<p><strong>I take anticoagulants — can the procedure still be carried out?</strong><br>Please tell us about the medicines you take. Anticoagulants must not be stopped without medical advice; where necessary we plan together with the relevant doctor.</p>
<p><strong>Is a bone graft needed for every implant?</strong><br>No. The need is determined by assessing the existing bone volume and the planned prosthesis together; in most cases no additional procedure is required.</p>
<p><strong>Can I go to work or school after the procedure?</strong><br>It depends on the extent of the procedure. Rest may be advised for the first day. Advice specific to you is given beforehand.</p>
<h2>Local assessment</h2>
<p>At our clinic at the Beylikdüzü–Esenyurt junction, the need for surgery is determined by assessing the clinical examination, the necessary imaging methods and the anatomical relationships together. Following the assessment, an appropriate treatment plan is agreed with the patient.</p>
<p><a href="/en/dental-implant-tedavisi/">Dental Implant Treatment</a> &middot; <a href="/en/kanal-tedavisi/">Root Canal Treatment</a> &middot; <a href="/en/iletisim">Appointments and contact</a></p>',
'Impacted wisdom teeth, surgical extraction, bone augmentation and sinus lifting: assessment, planning of the procedure and the healing process.',
'Oral and Maxillofacial Surgery | MSc. Dt. Mehmet Taş',
'EN' FROM pages WHERE link = 'cerrahi';

